// A status line for a shell prompt — the first consumer `@/packages/git`'s types have had, and the
// place where turning characters into an enum costs something.
//
//     main *2 +1 ?3
//
// A branch, then a count per kind of change. Every shell that shows git state shows roughly this,
// and it is the smallest program that has to **aggregate** a status rather than print it.

import { Vec } from "core";
import { Change, Entry } from "./status.wac";

/** What a prompt says. Counts rather than paths, which is the whole difference from `status`. */
export struct Summary {
  string branch;
  /** Files whose index differs from `HEAD`. */
  i32 staged;
  /** Files whose working tree differs from the index. */
  i32 dirty;
  /** Files git has never seen. */
  i32 untracked;
}

/**
 * Summarise, in the one walk there is.
 *
 * The two columns are independent — `@/packages/git/src/status.wac` keeps the shipped doc's reason
 * for that, *"a file staged and then edited again is `MM`, which is the case that proves they are
 * computed separately"* — so a file counts in `staged` **and** `dirty`, and this loop is where that
 * stops being a fact about porcelain and becomes a fact about the arithmetic.
 */
export Summary summarise(string branch, Vec<Entry> entries) {
  Summary s = Summary(branch, 0, 0, 0);
  for (Entry e in entries.items()) {
    if (e.staged is Untracked) { s.untracked = s.untracked + 1; continue; }
    if (!(e.staged is Unmodified))   { s.staged = s.staged + 1; }
    if (!(e.worktree is Unmodified)) { s.dirty = s.dirty + 1; }
  }
  return s;
}

/** `main *2 +1 ?3`, with absent counts omitted. */
export string render(const Summary s) { … }

// ## 1. The enum removed a character and counting puts it back
//
// This is the finding, and it took the second function to see it.
//
// A prompt that shows *how many of each kind* — `2 modified, 1 added, 1 deleted` — wants a count per
// `Change`. The obvious shape is `Map<Change, i32>`, and it cannot be written:
// `../../../DECISIONS.md` says **references are comparable but not hashable**, and
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` already has `Map.create()` taking nothing here against two funcrefs in the
// tree. An enum with payload-carrying variants is a reference, so it cannot be a key, and `Change`
// has no payloads today but is one declaration away from having them.
//
// Three ways to count by kind, and each gives something up:
//
//   * **Seven fields.** `struct Counts { i32 unmodified; i32 added; … }` — the flat table this
//     directory has now met four times, and the first where it is *forced* rather than inherited.
//     Adding a variant means adding a field and nothing checks the two lists against each other.
//   * **An array indexed by the variant's ordinal**, which needs the ordinal to be a thing a program
//     can name. `spec/spec/enums.md` gives `match`, `is` and construction, and no way to say *the
//     index of this variant* — which is what makes an enum a closed set rather than a small integer
//     with names on it, and is the property this file wants to borrow for one loop.
//   * **`Map<u8, i32>` keyed on `Change.code()`** — and that is the character. `status.wac` replaced
//     porcelain's alphabet with an enum, kept `code()` for *formatting*, and the first aggregating
//     consumer reaches for `code()` as a **key**. The enum is intact and the character is back, one
//     layer down, with the type system no longer looking at it.
//
// **Answered 2026-09-05, by writing `core/map.wac`'s body.** None of the three is needed. The
// contradiction that entry named — `Map.create()` taking nothing here against two funcrefs in the
// tree — was resolved by making the pair a value, `Key<K> { hash; eq; }`, and the piece that falls
// out of it is `Key.by`, a **projection**: `Key.by(Key.bytes(), (Change c) => c.code())` gives a
// `Key<Change>`, so this file writes `Map<Change, i32>` and the map is keyed on the `Change`.
//
// The character is still what gets hashed and that is now an implementation detail of one `Key`
// rather than the type of the map. Which is the whole of the difference: `code()` as a *key* put the
// character back in the type system's view, and `code()` inside a `Key<Change>` does not.
//
// Worth recording how it was found: `core/map.wac` did not add `by` because somebody asked. It added
// it because writing the map's body meant reading the two call sites that had worked around its
// absence — this one and `@/packages/box/src/lib/args.wac`'s *"`u8` is not a key"* — and **both
// workarounds were the same projection, spelled by hand, in packages that did not know it.**
//
// The three below are kept because the reasoning is what made the fourth findable.
//
// The third is what this file would have done and is why the first two are written out. It is not
// that the enum was wrong — a swapped `Added` and `Deleted` is still impossible, which is what it
// bought. It
// is that **a closed set you cannot enumerate is a closed set you cannot tabulate**, and tabulating
// is what the second consumer of any enum does.
//
// ## 2. And `Untracked` is in the wrong column, which only a consumer notices
//
// `summarise` opens with `if (e.staged is Untracked)`, and that reads oddly on purpose: porcelain
// writes `??` in **both** columns for an untracked file, so `Change` has an `Untracked` variant that
// can appear in a field named *the index against `HEAD`* — where it means neither *staged* nor
// *unstaged* but *not a tracked file at all*.
//
// So `Entry` has three states in two fields: staged, unstaged, and a third that occupies both and is
// neither. `enum Entry { Tracked(Change staged, Change worktree), Untracked(string path) }` says it,
// and costs the two-column symmetry that makes the rest of the type read like porcelain. Which is
// the choice: **follow the format, or follow what the format means.** `status.wac` followed the
// format, correctly, because it was rewriting a status; the first caller that aggregates is where
// the difference shows.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **A branch name that might not exist.** `Summary.branch` is a `string` and a detached HEAD has no
// branch — the shell prompt shows a short commit id instead. `string?` would say it and then every
// `render` starts with a null check for a case that is common enough to have a name. The honest type
// is `enum Head { On(string branch), Detached(string commit) }`, which is a third type this file
// would declare about a subject `@/packages/git` owns, and it is the shape the *shipped* `repo.wac`
// already has internally and does not export.
//
// **`Summary` is four fields and three are counts of the same kind of thing.** A swap between
// `staged` and `dirty` type-checks and produces a prompt that is wrong in the direction nobody
// checks — `@/packages/wacc/src/ast.wac`'s argument about adjacent same-typed fields, arriving in a
// four-field struct rather than a seven-field one. Named construction would fix the call site here
// and the payload version of the same problem is what that file is about.
